<h3>Victory Status by Time Control Category</h3>
<div class="chart-row">
  <div class="chart-box full">
    <h4>How Games End by Time Control Category</h4>
    <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="chartTCVictory"></canvas></div>
  </div>
</div>

<table>
<tr><th>Category</th><th>Games</th><th>Avg. Turns</th><th>Mate %</th><th>Resign %</th><th>Timeout %</th><th>Draw %</th><th>White Win %</th><th>Black Win %</th></tr>
{''.join(f'<tr><td>{cat}</td><td>{tc_cat_stats[cat]["total"]:,}</td><td>{tc_cat_stats[cat]["avg_turns"]}</td><td>{tc_cat_stats[cat]["victory_status"].get("mate", 0)}%</td><td>{tc_cat_stats[cat]["victory_status"].get("resign", 0)}%</td><td>{tc_cat_stats[cat]["victory_status"].get("outoftime", 0)}%</td><td>{tc_cat_stats[cat]["draw_pct"]}%</td><td>{tc_cat_stats[cat]["white_pct"]}%</td><td>{tc_cat_stats[cat]["black_pct"]}%</td></tr>' for cat in tc_cat_order if cat in tc_cat_stats)}
</table>

<h3>Top Individual Time Controls</h3>
<div class="chart-row">
  <div class="chart-box full">
    <h4>Game Length by Time Control</h4>
    <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="chartTCLength"></canvas></div>
  </div>
</div>

<table>
<tr><th>Time Control</th><th>Games</th><th>Avg. Turns</th><th>Mate %</th><th>Resign %</th><th>Timeout %</th></tr>
{''.join(f'<tr><td>{tc}</td><td>{top_tc_stats[tc]["total"]:,}</td><td>{top_tc_stats[tc]["avg_turns"]}</td><td>{top_tc_stats[tc]["victory_status"].get("mate", 0)}%</td><td>{top_tc_stats[tc]["victory_status"].get("resign", 0)}%</td><td>{top_tc_stats[tc]["victory_status"].get("outoftime", 0)}%</td></tr>' for tc in top_tcs)}
</table>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2 id="key-findings">Key Findings and Strategic Takeaways</h2>
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="finding">
  <strong>1. White's first-move advantage is consistent and measurable.</strong>
  White wins {white_win_rate_decisive}% of decisive games &mdash; a +{round(white_win_rate_decisive - black_win_rate_decisive, 1)}pp edge over Black. This advantage persists across every rating bracket, every time control, and every victory status, confirming that the initiative from moving first translates into a real, measurable edge in online chess.
</div>

<div class="finding">
  <strong>2. The white advantage is amplified at lower rating levels.</strong>
  Among players rated below 1200, White's edge reaches its peak. Stronger players are better at neutralizing the first-move advantage through sound opening preparation and defensive technique. Coaches working with beginners should prioritize Black-side opening repertoire to help close this gap early.
</div>

<div class="finding">
  <strong>3. Rating differential is the strongest predictor of outcome.</strong>
  With a correlation of {correlation} between absolute rating gap and higher-rated player winning, skill gap dominates over color. A 200+ point gap translates to roughly a {diff_impact.get('200-299', {}).get('pct', 'N/A')}% win rate for the stronger player. Even a 50&ndash;99 point edge shifts win probability to {diff_impact.get('50-99', {}).get('pct', 'N/A')}%.
</div>

<div class="finding">
  <strong>4. Resignation dominates over checkmate.</strong>
  {victory_status_pct.get('resign', 0)}% of games end by resignation vs. only {victory_status_pct.get('mate', 0)}% by checkmate. This reflects online chess culture: players resign when the position is lost rather than playing to mate. Timeout accounts for {victory_status_pct.get('outoftime', 0)}%, which is significant in faster time controls.
</div>

<div class="finding">
  <strong>5. Time control fundamentally reshapes how games end.</strong>
  Bullet and blitz formats see dramatically higher timeout rates and shorter games. Classical controls produce more draws and longer games with more checkmates. The draw rate climbs from roughly {tc_cat_stats.get('Bullet (1-3)', {}).get('draw_pct', 'N/A')}% in bullet to {tc_cat_stats.get('Classical (30+)', {}).get('draw_pct', 'N/A')}% in classical &mdash; players with more time find more resources to hold difficult positions.
</div>

<div class="finding">
  <strong>6. Opening choice matters, but no opening eliminates White's edge.</strong>
  Among the 15 most popular openings, all favor White to some degree. However, some openings narrow the gap significantly{f' &mdash; e.g., {black_favored[0][0]} gives Black a +{round(black_favored[0][2] - black_favored[0][1], 1)}pp edge' if black_favored else ''}. Players seeking to maximize their chances with Black should focus on these statistically favorable defenses rather than symmetrical approaches.
</div>

<div class="finding">
  <strong>7. Deeper opening preparation correlates with higher draw rates.</strong>
  Games with deeper opening play (11+ plies of book theory) show elevated draw rates, suggesting that when both players navigate well-known theory, the position tends toward equilibrium. This underscores the value of novelties and departing from main lines if playing for a win.
</div>

<div class="finding">
  <strong>8. Higher-rated games last longer.</strong>
  Average game length increases with rating tier. Players in the 2000+ bracket average {length_by_tier.get('2000+', {}).get('mean', 'N/A')} turns vs. {length_by_tier.get('<1000', {}).get('mean', 'N/A')} turns for sub-1000 players. Better players convert advantages more patiently and defend tenaciously, extending the game.
</div>

<p class="footnote">
  Data source: Lichess.org API via Kaggle (datasnaek/chess) &mdash; {total_games:,} games.
  All statistics computed directly from the dataset. Report generated programmatically.
</p>

</div>

